# Understand Bubble Mart (3): How deep is the moat?

After dismantling the growth engine in the previous article, this article answers a more fundamental question:

**Can Bubble Mart maintain its profits? **

In other words – what is a moat? How deep? How long can it last?

I break it down one by one.

---

## 1. IP matrix: the core asset

Let’s look at the data first.

| IP | Revenue in 2025 (100 million yuan) | Year-on-year growth | Ratio of total revenue |
|---|---|---|---|
| LABUBU | 141.6 | +366% | 38.1% |
| SKULLPANDA | 35.4 | +171% | 9.5% |
| CRYBABY | 29.3 | +151% | 7.9% |
| MOLLY | 29.0 | +38% | 7.8% |
| DIMOO | 27.8 | +205% | 7.5% |
| Star People | 20.6 | +1617% | 5.5% |

6 IPs have annual revenue exceeding 2 billion, and 17 IPs have annual revenue exceeding 100 million. The thickness of this IP pool is unparalleled in the global fashion industry.

More important are two things:

**First, IP can last forever. ** MOLLY was born in 2016, and its revenue will continue to grow until 2025. A plastic doll has lived for nearly 10 years and is still making money. This in itself is evidence of a moat.

**Second, IP incubation capabilities can be replicated. ** Xingxingren’s revenue in 2024 will be 120 million, which will soar to 2.06 billion in 2025, a 17-fold increase. LABUBU was still a niche character when it was signed in 2019, but five years later it became a global phenomenon-level IP. This shows that Bubble Mart does not rely on luck to hit a hit product, but has a methodology for screening, cultivating and amplifying IP.

**But the problem is also obvious:**

One IP of LABUBU accounts for 38% of the revenue. Concentration is too high. If LABUBU's popularity wanes and the next successor has not yet stepped up, there will be a dangerous window of time.

There is also a deeper hidden worry - the ceiling of "storyless IP".

The IP of Bubble Mart has no story, no world view, and no characters. What consumers like is an "image", not a "role".

Looking around the world, there is only one precedent for "storyless IP" that can last forever: Hello Kitty. Sanrio has turned a mouthless cat into a cultural symbol through fifty years of operation. But Hello Kitty is an exception, not the rule.

The good news is that Bubble Mart is already making up for this shortcoming. LABUBU's animated film has been signed with Sony Pictures, and the director is Paul King, who made Paddington Bear. If the movie is successful, LABUBU will be upgraded from a "trendy toy image" to a "cultural IP with a story", and its life cycle will be greatly extended.

Whether this step will work is the most important variable worth tracking in the next three years.

---

## 2. Channel control: something that competitors don’t have

Many people compare Bubble Mart with American trendy toy companies. On the surface, they are both doing fashionable things, but their business models are completely different.

| Dimension | Bubble Mart | American Fashion Toy Company |
|---|---|---|
| Channels | 630 global direct stores + 2,637 robot stores | Relying on third-party retail such as Walmart and Target |
| Production | Own supply chain management | Outsourcing OEM |
| Pricing power | Complete self-control | Price pressure from retailers |
| Gross profit margin | 72% | About 35% |

The core difference is clear at a glance: Bubble Mart controls the entire chain from design, production, logistics to retail.

The 630 directly operated stores are not only sales terminals, but also brand experience spaces. When consumers walk into a Bubble Mart store, everything from lighting to display to the ritual of unboxing is carefully designed. Retailers with a row of boxes on their shelves can’t give that experience.

The 2637 robot store is even more powerful. Open in corners of shopping malls, subway stations, and airports, with low rent, less labor, and 24-hour operation. Capillary-level contacts are rolled out at extremely low marginal costs.

Channels are asset-heavy and are slow to build, but once built, it is difficult for latecomers to catch up. This is Bubble Mart’s most real barrier compared to all competitors.

---

## 3. Brand Mind: Synonymous with Category

What is the strongest state of a brand? When consumers think of a category, the first thing that pops up in their minds is you.

"Trendy toys = Bubble Mart". In the Chinese market, this equal sign has already been established.

The data speaks for itself: Bubble Mart has a domestic market share of 21.8%, while the second-place TOP TOY has only 4.8%. The difference is not just a tiny bit, it is crushing.

Category synonymous status means customer acquisition costs are extremely low. Consumers who want to buy trendy toys can go directly to a Bubble Mart store without searching or comparing.

**But this mentality is still very thin overseas. ** The overseas outbreak of Bubble Mart relies more on LABUBU’s single-point breakthrough. Overseas consumers recognize LABUBU, not Bubble Mart. If the popularity of LABUBU subsides one day, will Bubble Mart’s overseas brand be able to catch up? There is still a long way to go from "one IP makes a brand popular" to "one brand can continue to popularize new IPs".

---

## 4. Management: the most easily overlooked hidden moat

Wang Ning, the founder of Bubble Mart, is 39 years old. Looking back at his key decisions over the past few years:

| Time | Decision | Result |
|---|---|---|
| 2019 | Signed with LABUBU | Became a global phenomenon IP in five years |
| 2022 | Going overseas against the trend | Overseas income increased 10 times in two years |
| 2023 | Category jump | Opening up the income ceiling |
| 2025 | Actively slow down | Maintain discipline while running |

At every critical node, he made the right choice. Especially the matter of taking the initiative to slow down - applying the brakes when income is soaring, requires extremely strong determination. The vast majority of founders will choose to accelerate expansion at this moment.

**But there is also a signal that cannot be ignored:** In October 2024, the senior management team collectively reduced its holdings and cashed out a total of approximately HK$1.7 billion. Underweighting doesn't necessarily mean bearish -- executives have legitimate needs too. However, the collective reduction of holdings and the large scale at least indicate that the management believes that the stock price at that time has fully reflected the short-term value.

---

## 5. The fragile side of the moat

After saying "what is there", we must say "what is missing".

**The plush category has low barriers. ** The fastest growing category of Bubble Mart is plush. But as for plush, factories in Yiwu can imitate it in minutes. Bubble Mart relies on brand premium and channel control, rather than the technical barriers of the product itself.

**Emotional consumption is naturally unpredictable. ** Consumers think LABUBU is cute today, but they may think another thing is more cute tomorrow. No matter how strong your IP incubation capabilities are, there is no guarantee that you will be successful every time.

**"No story IP" lacks emotional anchor. ** Why does Disney’s Mickey Mouse live 100 years? Because generations have grown up watching its cartoons. Bubble Mart's IP does not have such an anchor, and the relationship between consumers and MOLLY is more like a "good-looking decoration" than a "childhood partner."

**AI-generated personalized IP is a long-term threat. ** In the 10-year perspective, if AI allows everyone to generate their own unique fashion image, the "standardized IP + mass production" model will be impacted. That day is still far away, but worth keeping an eye on.

---

## Final judgment: There is a moat, but it is not deep.

| Moat Dimension | Strength | Durability |
|---|---|---|
| IP Matrix | Strong | Medium (depends on continued incubation) |
| Channel control | Strong | Strong (heavy asset barriers) |
| Brand mentality | Strong domestically, weak overseas | Medium |
| Management | Strong | Medium (depends on individual) |
| **Comprehensive** | **Medium to Strong** | **Determined in 3-5 years, to be verified in 10 years** |

Bubble Mart's moat is "systemic capabilities" rather than "structural monopoly".

What is structural monopoly? Maotai's moat is an unreplicable microbial environment along the Chishui River. No one else can imitate it. Tencent's moat is the social relationship chain of more than one billion people, and users cannot leave even if they want to.

Bubble Mart is not like this. Its moat is the product of IP matrix, channel network, brand mentality and management capabilities. Individually, each item is not indestructible, but together they form a system that is difficult for competitors to copy in the short term.

Is it enough to last 3-5 years? Most likely enough. Is it enough for 10 years? uncertain. It depends on whether the LABUBU movie can successfully inject narrative genes into the IP, whether the incubation success rate of the new IP can be maintained, and whether the overseas brand mentality can be upgraded from "recognizing IP" to "recognizing brand".

